#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-infer — the perms seat's decider: is this ask one the box may grant itself, and does the owner's intent cover it?

  cc-infer decide --json FILE|- [--record]   one ask in, one verdict out (stdout: one JSON line)
  cc-infer decide --ask TEXT [--record]      the same for a bare command with no evidence (so never a grant)
  cc-infer digest [--hours N]                the told-after lines for the owner's daily digest (cc-reconcile digest)
  cc-infer backtest FILE                     replay past asks; exit 1 if one the owner refused, perms refused or the
                                             record keeps for him (owner-only) comes out a GRANT
  cc-infer selfcheck

The perms seat calls it, and so does the approver (`cc-approve decide …` runs this). It returns GRANT, GRANT+WATCH,
ASK-OWNER or REFUSE with its reasons and evidence; exit 0 on a GRANT or GRANT+WATCH, 1 otherwise, 2 on usage. ASK-OWNER
means the box doesn't grant it itself: perms decides, for the owner. It fails closed: anything it can't read, parse or
resolve is not a grant.

A GRANT comes only from a closed set of command shapes, parsed from the ask's whole text, never matched against prose
(architect, 10-11: "An ask that doesn't parse to one of those shapes goes to perms (ASK) whatever its wording, kind or
evidence. Free text never reaches GRANT."). The shapes, and nothing else:

  cc-config set KEY N                  KEY one of CONFIG_KEYS below, N a whole number in that key's range with no
                                       leading zero: how much one cc-ask spends and waits (the same bounds as
                                       cc-approve's KEY_OK). Nothing that lands, reviews, sets the tier, routes, pages, or touches the guard, the sandbox
                                       or perms. Undo: KEY back to its value before (`cc-config set KEY OLD`, or
                                       `cc-config unset KEY` if it had none). When the old value can't be read, the
                                       caller's own undo stands only if it parses as this shape; else no runnable
                                       undo is recorded, only a note saying why (a watcher runs the undo unattended).
  systemctl --user restart cc-NAME     restart one of the box's own user units. Restart only: stop, disable and mask
                                       are hard lines. Undo: none to run; the unit runs as before. Only for a unit
                                       `systemctl --user is-active` reads as active now: a restart starts an inactive
                                       one, and its undo would be a stop, a hard line, so that (or a state it can't
                                       read) goes to perms.

Rules, deterministic and in this order, so the backtest replays them exactly:

  1. REFUSE an empty ask, one the record shows the owner already said no to (`owner_refused`), and port 22 anywhere
     in it: that is SSH into the host.
  2. ASK-OWNER when the text still holds non-Latin letters after folding (NFKC, zero-width marks dropped, look-alike
     Cyrillic and Greek letters mapped to Latin, accents stripped), so the rules can't read it.
  3. ASK-OWNER for the hard lines, whatever a goal says: real money; secrets and credentials; the never-touch list;
     unplanned deletion of shared state; widening a member or any non-owner; mail, posts or anything outward; opening
     the HOST (SSH, any port, a connector or tunnel into a shell); the box's own controls, perms' standing condition
     (perms' CLAUDE.md or settings, ~/.cc/approvals, hooks, the guard, the sandbox or bwrap, autoMode, the tier, a
     shell profile, ~/bin, tmux or cc-msg into a seat, any cc-config key off the list); code fetched or decoded from
     outside the box; docker or any container run; a cron-style or every-N schedule; stopping or disabling a daemon;
     a push or merge (its target can't be read off prose, and a bare push goes to the default branch); and reading,
     writing or copying ~/.cc/config or ~/.cc/env. The folded text is matched whatever the `kind` says, and a hard
     kind is enough alone. Each reason names the word or kind that matched. Not matched, here or for the ports of rule
     1: a negated clause of plain words that ends with its clause ("no secrets, no member widening"), a Z-stamped or
     dated time ("03:21Z"), and a ref ending in a default branch ("origin/main"); a verb beside them still matches. These are an extra refusal layer: rule 4
     is what keeps a rewording out.
  4. ASK-OWNER unless the whole ask parses to one of the shapes above.
  5. ASK-OWNER with no resolved evidence of his intent that shares a content word with the ask: no evidence, no grant.
  6. `reaches` and `reversible` are claims, and only a stricter one counts: a claim past the owner, or that it can't
     be undone, is ASK-OWNER. GRANT when it undoes in minutes; GRANT+WATCH, with the shape's undo, when it claims hours.

Owner, 10-11: the perms seat must infer low-level permissions from his high-level goals and weigh each against its
actual risk, "because many things can be reverted". His example was a client's test site, which waited on his yes to
go public though his wish for it was in Slack and its subdomain was already planned. A public site has no parsed shape
here yet, so it goes to perms.

THE ASK (JSON). `ask` is required, and is the command itself; every other key is optional.
  kind        a claim; mail|post|money|secret|never-touch|delete-shared|widen-member|open-host|controls keep it with the
              owner on their own, and any other value changes nothing (the shape is what decides)
  reaches     box|owner|member|public|outside-person   only a value past the owner counts
  reversible  minutes|hours|no                         hours makes a grant watched; no keeps it with the owner
  money       true keeps it with the owner;  data  secret keeps it with the owner
  evidence    [{"quote": "...", "source": "..."}]. A quote counts when it is at least 20 characters, shares a content
              word with the ask (not a stopword, a shape's own word or a repo name), and is found for real in one
              line of one of these, a heading or a line under 20 characters never counting:
                ~/.cc/state/<repo>/goals.md, where <repo> is one of the owner's own repos (CC_JANITOR_REPOS in his
                ~/.cc/config, read through cc-config with HOME, CC_CONFIG and CC_CONFIG_DENY pinned) and not a member
                workspace (~/.cc/members/<repo> without its owner-workspace marker); never a goals.md under ~/dev/
                or anywhere else;
                grant:N, item N of the owner's one-time grant in docs/host-rules.md.
              A cc-lib ticket is no evidence: tickets.log records no message, so its quote can't be tied to what it
              answered. ~ is the account's home from the password database, never $HOME, and only selfcheck() swaps
              these places, and the ledger's, for scratch ones, so the caller can't choose the evidence.
  owner_refused  where the record shows he refused it;  check  how a watcher sees it is fine (the undo is the shape's, above)

--record writes, for a GRANT or GRANT+WATCH only: one line in the ledger (~/.cc/state/perms/inferred.jsonl: what,
evidence, watcher, revert, revert_note) and, for GRANT+WATCH, a watcher registration in ~/.cc/state/perms/watch-spool/<id>.json. The watcher framework (row perms-grant-watchers) has not landed, so the spool is the interface until it does:
one JSON object per file with id, created, what, check, revert, revert_note, expires and evidence. revert is a
command a watcher may run, or "" when there is none, and then revert_note says why. The owner sees the ledger in the
11:00 digest as told-after, never as a card, with the ask text escaped so it can't ping or link in Slack.
"""
import json, os, pwd, re, shlex, subprocess, sys, time, unicodedata

# the account's own home, never $HOME: the caller must not choose where evidence is read from (security read, 10-11)
HOME = pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_dir
USAGE = "usage: cc-infer decide (--json FILE|- | --ask TEXT) [--record] | digest [--hours N] | backtest FILE | selfcheck"
GRANTS = ("GRANT", "GRANT+WATCH")
WATCH_DAYS = 7


# Stand-ins for the places evidence, the owner's repos and the ledger come from. Only selfcheck() sets them; a normal
# run reads no environment for them, so whoever runs the tool can't choose its evidence (security read, 10-11: "CC_INFER_*
# path overrides take effect only inside selfcheck(); in a normal run they're ignored").
_TEST = {}


def place(key, default):
    return _TEST[key] if key in _TEST else default


def state_dir():
    return place("dir", f"{HOME}/.cc/state/perms")


# The closed set of command shapes (architect, 10-11: "GRANT comes only from a closed, enumerated set of command
# shapes, parsed, not pattern-matched against prose"). cc-config keys: how much one cc-ask spends and waits, held in
# the ranges cc-approve's KEY_OK holds them in. A key joins by a reviewed PR, never by an ask.
CONFIG_KEYS = {"CC_ASK_TURNS": (1, 24), "CC_ASK_TIMEOUT": (30, 300), "CC_ASK_THINK": (0, 10000),
               "CC_ASK_PACKET": (1024, 16384)}
SHAPES = [("config", re.compile(r"cc-config set (" + "|".join(CONFIG_KEYS) + r") (0|[1-9]\d{0,5})")),
          ("service", re.compile(r"systemctl --user restart (cc-[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*)(?:\.service)?"))]
SHAPE_WORDS = {"cc", "config", "set", "systemctl", "user", "restart", "service"}
REACH_RANK = {"box": 0, "owner": 1, "member": 2, "public": 3, "outside-person": 4}
UNDO_RANK = {"minutes": 0, "hours": 1, "no": 2}
L, R = r"(?<![a-z0-9])", r"(?![a-z0-9])"   # word edges that also split on _ and -, so GH_TOKEN reads as a token


def W(words):
    return L + "(?:" + words + ")" + R


def parse_shape(text):
    """(kind, what, key) when the ask's whole text, stripped, is one shape; else None. Only printable ASCII and
    single spaces, so a second line, a tab or a look-alike letter never parses."""
    s = str(text or "").strip()
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[ -~]+", s) or "  " in s:
        return None
    for kind, rx in SHAPES:
        m = rx.fullmatch(s)
        if not m:
            continue
        if kind == "config":
            lo, hi = CONFIG_KEYS[m.group(1)]
            if not lo <= int(m.group(2)) <= hi:
                return None
            return kind, f"{m.group(1)}={int(m.group(2))}", m.group(1)
        return kind, m.group(1), None
    return None


UNSET = object()


def config_now(key):
    """KEY's value now, through cc-config with its environment pinned: the value, UNSET (get exits 1: no value), or
    None when it can't be read (any other exit, a timeout, no cc-config)."""
    if "config" in _TEST:
        return _TEST["config"].get(key, UNSET)
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([place("cc_config", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-config")),
                            "get", key], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20, env=config_env(),
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return p.stdout.rstrip("\n") if p.returncode == 0 else UNSET if p.returncode == 1 else None


def caller_undo(revert, key):
    """The caller's own revert as words when it is exactly an undo of KEY that the config shape itself would accept
    (`cc-config unset KEY`, or `cc-config set KEY N` with N a plain number in KEY's range), else None. A watcher runs
    the revert on its own, so an unchecked value never reaches it (planning's review of #1089: "Keep it only if
    parse_shape(shlex.join(words)) passes")."""
    try:
        words = shlex.split(squash(revert))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if words == ["cc-config", "unset", key]:
        return words
    shape = parse_shape(shlex.join(words)) if len(words) == 4 and words[1:3] == ["set", key] else None
    return words if shape and shape[2] == key else None


def unit_state(unit):
    """What `systemctl --user is-active cc-NAME.service` says now: "active", another state word, or "" when it can't
    be read. Read-only, and the binary is pinned, so a caller's PATH can't fake an active unit. Lander review of #1089:
    a restart of an inactive unit starts it, so "the unit runs as before" holds only for an active one."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([place("systemctl", "/usr/bin/systemctl"), "--user", "is-active", f"{unit}.service"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""
    s = p.stdout.strip()
    return s if re.fullmatch(r"[a-z-]{1,20}", s) and (s == "active") == (p.returncode == 0) else ""


def shape_undo(shape, revert):
    """(revert, note) for a grant: revert is a command a watcher may run, or "" when there is none to run; note says
    why when it is "". A restart has nothing to undo. A config key goes back to its value now (lane review of #1089:
    "If the key already had a value, restore that value instead"); the caller's own checked undo stands when it agrees,
    or when the value now can't be read. With neither, nothing runnable is recorded: tell, don't auto-revert."""
    if shape[0] != "config":
        return "", "nothing to undo: a restart of an active unit leaves it running as it was"
    key, old = shape[2], config_now(shape[2])
    theirs = caller_undo(revert, key)
    if old is None:
        if theirs:
            return shlex.join(theirs), ""
        return "", f"{key}'s value before couldn't be read, so no undo is recorded; check it by hand before undoing"
    mine = f"cc-config unset {key}" if old is UNSET else f"cc-config set {key} {shlex.quote(old)}"
    return mine, ""


# The hard lines, matched on the folded text (fold() below). Each is (name, pattern); a kind maps onto the same names.
HARD = [
    ("real money", r"[$€£¥]\s?\d|\d\s?[$€£¥]|" + W(r"usd|eur|euros?|dollars?|pounds?|gbp|cents?|payments?|pay|paid|paying|"
                   r"purchases?|buy|buying|order from|invoices?|subscriptions?|subscribe|credit card|debit|card on file|"
                   r"charge|charged|charges|billing|bill|refund|renewal|renew|spend|spending|budget|prices?|wallet|"
                   r"crypto|bitcoin|stripe|paypal|live (broker|trading|account)|real account|acquire|acquiring|amazon|orders?|"
                   r"ordering|checkout")),
    ("secrets and credentials", r"token|credential|passw|passwd|secret|api[ _-]?keys?|private[ _-]?keys?|ssh[ _-]?keys?|"
